invariant: INV-26
file: aes_hw.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh aes-equiv
reason: the x86-64 AES-256 key expansion rotates the temporary of the step
  every second round key takes, where FIPS 197 applies SubWord alone. That
  step is the one AES-128 does not have, so only the AES-256 schedules
  change. bin/aes_equiv_test compares each schedule with the table's byte
  for byte, over keys whose second half is all that differs. Only an
  x86-64 compiler reads the line, so the script builds that binary for
  x86-64 and runs it under qemu-x86_64.

--- old
    return _mm_xor_si128(prefix_xor(key), sub_word_of_last_word(first));
--- new
    return _mm_xor_si128(prefix_xor(key), rot_word_each(sub_word_of_last_word(first)));
